// ===== UPDATE 2026-07-20 (v-iterators) — ROOT CAUSE SHARPENED (v-core-opt re-diagnosed + I re-verified on
// TRUNK b140ef9c8). The "2-hop-forward" framing below is INCOMPLETE; the real discriminator is REACHABILITY,
// and the check-vs-compile DIVERGENCE PERSISTS on trunk for the reachable case: =====
//   - REACHABLE consumer (export { run }, run=sum(from-list([1,2,3]))): `cdz compile` exit 0 + runs 6, but
//     `cdz check` exit 1 (CDZ0201). <-- check != compile ON TRUNK (contradicts "exported already works").
//   - DEAD consumer (export { Iter.* }, sum unexported): BOTH `cdz check` and `cdz compile` exit 1.
//   - The actual gate case, an `@test` calling sum: `cdz check` exit 1 (this is what reds the package).
//   v-core-opt: reachability drives COMPILE (dead fails standalone const-elab; reachable specializes at its
//   call site) — but `cdz check`/`check_one` rejects BOTH reachable AND dead. So a DEAD-CODE-ONLY compile fix
//   will NOT unred the gate (the gate runs check, and @tests are reachable-yet-check-rejected). Fix must make
//   `cdz check` agree with `cdz compile` for the REACHABLE const-wrapper-chain consumer. (Note: can't just
//   export the wrapper — `sum(it)`'s `it` param is not boundary-representable, a separate CDZ0201.)
//   v-iterators gate-unblock (MR 3b6cc35b8, sum/product call drive directly) stands until this lands.
// =============================================================================================================
//
// BUG (filed by v-iterators 2026-07-20, trunk f9c076ee1): `cdz check` FALSE-REJECTS with CDZ0201
// "an argument to a const parameter must be compile-time-known — it depends on runtime data" a program
// that `cdz compile` ACCEPTS and RUNS correctly. It is a CHECK-vs-COMPILE divergence: `check_one`'s
// const-provability analysis loses const-ness when a literal closure is forwarded through TWO wrapper
// levels into a `const` param, but the real compile path (monomorphization/specialization) handles it fine.
//
// WHY IT MATTERS NOW: the check-first `cdz test` gate (5a44da08f, landed f9c076ee1) runs `check_one` BEFORE
// the suite and fails RED on any check error. So this check false-positive turns a green, compiling,
// running package RED in the fleet cdz-test gate. It red'd the whole iterators package (every @test flowing
// through sum/product); I unblocked iterators by inlining the wrapper (sum/product drive directly, one hop),
// but the check bug will bite ANY vertical using a `const`-param wrapper chain >=2 deep. Filing per the
// idiomatic-code directive (the inline was a pragmatic gate-unblock; this is the real bug).
//
// EXACT DIVERGENCE (same file): `cdz check` exit 1 (CDZ0201 at the sum(...) call site) vs `cdz compile`
// exit 0, `cdz run --call run` => 6. Verified on trunk f9c076ee1.
//
// BISECTED TRIGGER (hop count from the literal closure to the `const` param):
//   - literal -> const param DIRECTLY (0 wrapper hops): CLEAN.
//   - literal -> 1 wrapper -> const param (e.g. a @test calling `fold` directly): CLEAN.
//   - literal created in `sum`, -> `fold` -> `drive const g` (2 wrapper hops below the literal): REJECTED.
//   Predicate consumers (any/all/find: forward their OWN param one hop into `const p`) are CLEAN — so it is
//   specifically a literal closure losing const-provability past the 2nd forwarding hop, NOT param-forwarding
//   per se. `const`-annotating the intermediate wrapper param does NOT help (still rejected).
//
// LIKELY LOCUS: `check_one`'s const-argument provability (the front-end/type-check severity path the gate
// uses) does not propagate "this arg originates from a compile-time-known literal" across two def-call
// boundaries, whereas the compile path's specialization does. Routing to concierge for owner assignment
// (cdz-check const-analysis: v-cdz-tooling owns the `cdz` CLI/check surface; v-core-opt owns const-elaboration).

type Iter = | Mk(List(Int64), List(Int64) -> Option((Int64, List(Int64))))
def from-list(xs) = Iter.Mk(xs, fn(s) => match s with | [] => Option.None(unit) | [h, .. t] => Option.Some((h, t)))
def drive(const step: List(Int64) -> Option((Int64, List(Int64))), s: List(Int64), acc: Int64, const g: Int64 -> Int64 -> Int64) =
  match step(s) with | Option.None(_) => acc | Option.Some(p) => (match p with | (x, s2) => drive(step, s2, g(acc, x), g))
def fold(it, acc, g: Int64 -> Int64 -> Int64) = match it with | Iter.Mk(s, step) => drive(step, s, acc, g)
// sum creates the literal `fn(a,x)=>a+x` and forwards it through fold->drive (2 hops) -> `cdz check` CDZ0201,
// but `cdz compile` accepts + runs (value 6). THE BUG.
def sum(it) = fold(it, 0, fn(a, x) => a + x)
def run() = sum(from-list([1, 2, 3]))

// CONTROL — a @test calling fold DIRECTLY (literal one hop into fold, one more into drive = clean): both
// check and compile accept. Distinguishes the 2-wrapper-hop trigger from param-forwarding in general.
@test
def fold-direct-one-hop-is-clean() =
  if fold(from-list([3, 4, 5]), 0, fn(a, x) => a + x) == 12 then unit else trap("fold direct")

export { Iter.* }
